\begin{observation}
	\label{obs: auxiliary cycles non-transversal at at most one}
	For every index $1\leq z<r$, for every pair $e, e'\in \hat E_z$ of distinct edges, there is at most one vertex $v\in V(W(e))\cap V(W(e'))$, such that the  intersection of cycles $W(e)$ and $W(e')$ is transversal at $v$. If such a vertex $v$ exists, then $v\in S_j$ for some index $z< j<r$, and either $j-1$ is the last index in both $\spann''(e),\spann''(e')$, or $j-1$ is the last index in one of these sets, while $j$ belongs to another.
